@*
    OdsPasswordRules — the live password-requirement checklist
    (Odyssey Design System · components/PasswordRules).

    The one place the five rules are rendered. Register, /account's change-password
    section and /reset-password all mount THIS component, reading the rules from the
    single PasswordPolicy, so the displayed requirements can't drift apart (issue #405).

    Met state is conveyed by icon + text, never colour alone, and each row carries an
    off-screen "met / not yet met" phrase so a screen reader hears the state too.

    A host disables its submit button from PasswordPolicy.IsSatisfied(password) — the
    same call that renders these ticks. (The design system exposes that as the
    component's `allMet`; in Blazor the host reads the policy directly rather than
    reaching into a child component through @@ref.)
*@

<ul class="odc-pw-rules @(Columns == 2 ? "cols-2" : null) @Class" aria-label="@AriaLabel">
    @foreach (var rule in PasswordPolicy.Rules(Password))
    {
        <li class="odc-pw-rule @(rule.Met ? "met" : null)" @key="rule.Key">
            <MudIcon Icon="@(rule.Met ? Icons.Material.Filled.CheckCircle : Icons.Material.Outlined.RadioButtonUnchecked)"
                     Size="Size.Small"
                     aria-hidden="true" />
            <span>@rule.Label</span>
            <span class="sr-only">@(rule.Met ? " — met" : " — not yet met")</span>
        </li>
    }
</ul>

@code {
    /// <summary>The candidate password, evaluated live.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Password { get; set; }

    /// <summary>1 (stacked, the default — fits the 420px auth card) or 2 (grid, for wide forms).</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Columns { get; set; } = 1;

    /// <summary>Overrides the list's accessible name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Password requirements";

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
